## Brief Overview

---

Audio system calibration, at Malaga Event Gear, means setting every microphone and speaker to the room it is actually in, not just plugging equipment in and turning it on. The same PA and console sound different in a low ceilinged indoor hall than they do in an open garden, and different again once a room fills with guests, so calibration is the process our crew runs at every single booking to make the equipment fit that specific venue.

This guide walks through what calibration actually involves, gain staging in plain terms, why room acoustics, indoor versus outdoor and guest count all change what correct calibration looks like, and the real console range our crew calibrates with, from a 6 channel entry desk up to a Midas M32R Live digital system most guests will never see. [Contact us](/contact/) if you want to know what that looks like for your specific venue.

## Key Highlights

---

-   Calibration is not a generic checklist. It is gain staging on every microphone, speaker placement, and console settings, all adjusted to the specific room, not a fixed setting we bring to every event.

-   Gain staging, setting each input's level correctly at the source, has to be redone at every venue because the microphones, the distances and the room itself all change.

-   Room acoustics, indoor versus outdoor, and guest count all change what correct calibration means for identical equipment. A full room absorbs sound differently than the same empty room during setup.

-   Our console range scales with the event: a 6 channel Soundcraft EPM-6 for smaller bookings, a 16 channel Mackie Onyx 1640 for mid size corporate events, and a Midas M32R Live paired with an MD16 digital stage box on larger venues.

-   This page is strictly about audio calibration. Screen and presentation switching is a separate topic, covered in full on our [video switcher rental](/blog/video-switcher-rental/) guide.

## What Audio System Calibration Actually Means

Calibration is the work our crew does between arriving at your venue and your first guest walking in: adjusting every microphone's input level, checking speaker placement against the room's shape, and setting the console so the whole system sits at a level that sounds correct in that specific space, neither too quiet to be heard in the back row nor loud enough to distort or trigger feedback near the front. It is not a single setting configured in advance and brought unchanged to every event. The Product Presentation Pack's mixing console, for example, gets set differently for a small dealership showroom than it does for a larger conference hall, even though the physical unit is identical.

The reason calibration cannot be skipped or standardized is simple: a speaker's level and a microphone's gain only sound correct relative to the room they are in. Move the exact same equipment to a different room, and what was correct becomes either too weak or too hot. Calibration is the process of finding correct again, every time.

## Gain Staging in Plain Terms

Gain staging is the part of calibration most event hosts have never heard named, even though it is the single biggest factor in whether a microphone sounds clean or not. Every microphone and audio input has a level, called gain, set right at the source, before the signal reaches the console's main volume or the speakers at all.

Set that gain too low, and the microphone sounds weak and distant, forcing the console's overall volume up to compensate, which drags up any background hiss or room noise right along with the voice. Set it too high, and the signal distorts, a harsh, crackling sound called clipping, the moment the presenter raises their voice or steps closer to the mic. Correct gain staging sits between those two, loud enough to sound present without ever pushing the signal into distortion.

<InlineCTA />

Gain staging has to be redone at every event because the variables genuinely change. A wired podium microphone in a quiet boardroom picks up almost nothing but the speaker's voice, so its gain can run comfortably high. The same style of microphone on an outdoor stage, further from the presenter's mouth and competing with ambient noise, needs a different setting entirely. A wireless handheld microphone passed between wedding guests changes distance constantly, so our crew sets its gain with headroom built in rather than tuned tight to one fixed position. None of this is guesswork, it is the reason our crew arrives and tests before your event starts rather than trusting a setting from the last booking.

## Why Every Venue Needs Different Calibration

Room acoustics change what correct calibration looks like even when the equipment stays identical. A room with a low ceiling and hard surfaces, tile floors, glass walls, bare stone, reflects sound back into the space quickly. Those reflections reach a microphone almost as fast as the direct sound from a speaker, which raises the risk of feedback, the rising squeal a mic makes when it picks up its own amplified sound. In that kind of room, our crew sets gain lower and angles speakers more carefully than in a taller room with soft furnishings, carpet, drapes, a full set of upholstered chairs, which absorb sound rather than bouncing it back.

Ceiling height matters for the same reason. A high, open ceiling gives sound somewhere to dissipate rather than reflect straight back down into the seating area, so the same speaker level that would cause problems in a low conference room can sit comfortably in a taller hall. None of this shows up by looking at the equipment list. It only shows up by standing in the actual room.

## Indoor, Outdoor and Guest Count: What Actually Changes

Outdoor venues remove the reflections indoor calibration accounts for, and replace them with a different problem: open air and wind carry sound away instead of returning it. There are no walls to reinforce a speaker's output, so our crew places speakers closer to the audience and often runs gain higher for the same coverage than the equivalent indoor setup would need, since there is no reflected sound filling in the gaps between speaker positions. The cover photo on this page, an outdoor conference setup for High Care Hospital in Marbella under a partially shaded patio, shows exactly this: the speaker sits close to the rows of seating rather than trying to throw sound across open garden the way an indoor PA might.

Guest count changes calibration in a way that is easy to miss entirely: a full room of people, their bodies and clothing, absorbs sound differently than the same room empty during setup. That is a real, physical difference, not a rounding error, which is why our crew tests as close to your doors opening as practically possible rather than calibrating hours ahead in an empty room and assuming it will hold once guests fill the seats.

