## The Midas M32R Live and MD16: Calibration on Larger Venues

Our largest console pairing has never been named on this site before now: a Midas M32R Live digital mixing console, paired with an MD16 digital stage box, both confirmed in our own equipment inventory. This is the setup our crew reaches for on larger venues, the scale a [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) booking with up to 120 guests actually needs.

What makes the MD16 worth explaining rather than just naming is what it does differently from a standard console setup. On a smaller booking, every microphone cable runs individually, in a bundle, all the way from the stage back to wherever the console sits. On a larger venue that can mean a long run of cable snaking across the floor or taped down along a wall. The MD16 changes that: it is a remote stage box that sits near the microphones themselves, on or close to the stage, and converts each one to a digital signal right there. A single cable then carries all of those channels back to the M32R Live console, wherever our crew positions it in the room, instead of a long bundle of individual microphone cables making the same trip.

That matters for calibration in two concrete ways. First, converting each microphone's signal close to the source, with the preamps built into the stage box rather than back at the console, keeps the signal clean over a longer physical distance, since it is not traveling as a lower level analog signal picking up interference the whole way. Second, it gives our crew genuine channel by channel control on a larger room, shaping each microphone individually rather than sharing broad tone adjustments across grouped inputs the way a smaller analog desk like the EPM-6 has to. On a room built for 120 guests, that level of individual control is exactly what venue by venue calibration, the whole subject of this page, actually depends on.

