## How Far in Advance to Book

All our bookings need at least 24 hours' notice, and calibration is part of every single one of them regardless of package. Larger venues that call for the Midas M32R Live and MD16 stage box benefit from more lead time, not because calibration itself takes longer, but because confirming a larger room's layout and guest count ahead of the day lets our crew plan speaker placement and cable runs before arriving rather than improvising on site. Demand for our corporate packages peaks during the conference season and around major congresses on the Costa del Sol, so confirming early matters most for [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) bookings specifically.

## Conclusion

Audio system calibration at Malaga Event Gear is not a fixed setting, it is gain staging and speaker placement adapted to the room your event is actually in, room acoustics, indoor versus outdoor, and guest count all changing what correct calibration means for identical equipment. Our console range scales with that, from a 6 channel Soundcraft EPM-6 up to a Midas M32R Live paired with an MD16 digital stage box on larger venues, named here for the first time on this site. [Contact us](/contact/) with your venue and guest count, and we will tell you honestly what calibration looks like for it. For the phase by phase version of what our crew does on site, see our [technical support for events](/blog/technical-support-for-events/) guide. For our full equipment range, see the [audio visual rental in Malaga guide](/blog/audio-visual-rental/).

## FAQs

### What is audio system calibration, in plain terms?

Setting every microphone and speaker to the specific room your event is in, rather than plugging equipment in and turning it on. It covers gain staging on each input, speaker placement, and console settings, all adjusted to that room's acoustics, not a fixed configuration brought from the last event. See [What Audio System Calibration Actually Means](#what-audio-system-calibration-actually-means) above.

### What is gain staging, and why does it matter?

Gain staging is setting each microphone's input level correctly at the source, before it reaches the console's main volume. Too low, and the signal sounds weak and picks up background noise once the volume is pushed up to compensate. Too high, and it distorts the moment a presenter raises their voice. See [Gain Staging in Plain Terms](#gain-staging-in-plain-terms) above.

### Why does the same equipment need different calibration at different venues?

Room acoustics change what correct calibration looks like even with identical gear. A low ceiling and hard surfaces, tile, glass, bare stone, reflect sound back quickly and raise feedback risk, so gain runs lower and speaker angles are set more carefully than in a taller room with soft, sound absorbing furnishings. See [Why Every Venue Needs Different Calibration](#why-every-venue-needs-different-calibration) above.

### Does an outdoor venue get calibrated differently than an indoor one?

Yes. Outdoor venues have no walls to reflect sound back the way indoor rooms do, so speakers get placed closer to the audience and gain often runs higher for the same coverage, since there is no reflected sound filling gaps between speaker positions. See [Indoor, Outdoor and Guest Count](#indoor-outdoor-and-guest-count-what-actually-changes) above.

### Does guest count affect calibration, and if so how?

Yes. A full room of guests, their bodies and clothing, absorbs sound differently than the same room empty during setup. That is why our crew tests as close to your doors opening as practically possible rather than calibrating hours ahead in an empty room. See [Indoor, Outdoor and Guest Count](#indoor-outdoor-and-guest-count-what-actually-changes) above.

### What mixing console does MEG use for calibration, and does it depend on the package?

Yes, it scales with event size: a 6 channel Soundcraft EPM-6 for smaller bookings with a console, such as the [Product Presentation Pack](/packages/product-presentation/), a 16 channel Mackie Onyx 1640 for mid size corporate events, and a Midas M32R Live with an MD16 digital stage box on larger venues, up to the [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/)'s 120 guests. The [Eco Pack](/packages/eco/) itself has no console at all. See [Same Real Equipment, Calibrated for Scale](#same-real-equipment-calibrated-for-scale-our-console-range) above.

### What is the Midas M32R Live and MD16 stage box, and when does MEG use it?

The Midas M32R Live is our digital mixing console for larger venues, paired with an MD16, a remote stage box that converts each microphone to digital right at the stage and carries every channel back to the console over a single cable, instead of a long bundle of individual microphone cables. We use this pairing on larger bookings, the scale our [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) serves up to 120 guests. See [The Midas M32R Live and MD16](#the-midas-m32r-live-and-md16-calibration-on-larger-venues) above.

### Does calibration include video or screen setup too?

No, not on this page. Audio calibration, gain staging and speaker tuning, is a separate process from screen and presentation source switching, which our [Kramer VP-731](/blog/video-switcher-rental/) handles. If your event's account mentions calibrating "sound and video networks" together, the video half refers to that presentation scaler, not a camera or broadcast system, which we do not offer. See [Calibration in the Field](#calibration-in-the-field-the-bmotion-marbella-project) above.

### Is calibration done once, or does it change during the event?

Calibration itself happens once, before your guests arrive, setting the baseline for your event. What can change afterward, a cable getting knocked loose or a gain fader nudged, is a separate question on the self run packages where no technician is watching the levels. On packages with a technician, live monitoring keeps adjusting from that calibrated baseline as your event runs.

### Do I need to be there while my equipment is calibrated?

No. Our crew calibrates every booking before you or your guests arrive, as part of the delivery, install and test process on every package. You do not need to be present for it, though you are welcome to be if your schedule allows.

### How far in advance does calibration happen relative to my event start time?

Our crew arrives and calibrates ahead of your first guest, timed close enough to your doors opening that guest count and room absorption are accounted for, not hours earlier when the room is still empty. See [Indoor, Outdoor and Guest Count](#indoor-outdoor-and-guest-count-what-actually-changes) and [How Far in Advance to Book](#how-far-in-advance-to-book) above for what that means for booking lead time.
